From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tiberiu Chibici <chibicitiberiu@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 11:00:00 +0300
Subject: ps3: sys-manager: fan policy through the System Manager (hwmon pwm1)

SYSCON keeps a fan policy set by a guest in its RAM after the guest is
gone: a manual duty webMAN set in GameOS stays in force under Linux, and
nothing in Linux could hand the fan back to SYSCON. Now, with
CONFIG_PS3_SYS_MANAGER_HWMON, the driver uses the System Manager's
FAN_POLICY service (25 set, 50 get; GameOS's sys_sm_set_fan_policy and
sys_sm_get_fan_policy, lv2 syscalls 389 and 409):

- At boot (a work item, 0.5 s after probe) it logs the policy GameOS
  left (a get), sets SYSCON automatic, and then full speed or a manual
  floor if asked for (ps3_sys_manager.fan_policy=auto|keep|full|off,
  ps3_sys_manager.fan_floor=<duty>).
- hwmon ps3_sm gets pwm1_enable (0 full speed, 1 manual, 2 SYSCON
  automatic) and pwm1 (the manual duty). A manual duty is fixed, with no
  control loop, and always clamped to 0x99-0xf4 (60-95%): it must be safe
  under a game's load, and 0x99 is twice SYSCON's own curve on a CECH-20xx
  up to about 70 C (psdevwiki Thermal) and the fixed speed the project's
  rules already accept.
- SYSCON automatic again on every exit path when anything else was set:
  .shutdown (power off, reboot, halt, kexec) under the request mutex
  (bounded wait for it), the final power off and restart routines, and a
  panic notifier at INT_MAX (on the PS3, ps3_panic() at INT_MIN never
  returns, so it is the only restore after a panic). The lockless ones use
  a new ps3_vuart_write_atomic(): trylock on the tx lock, nothing queued
  before, the whole message written in a bounded spin, no allocation.
  fan_dirty goes up before full/manual is written and down only after
  automatic was, so no exit path misses a setting; it starts up, so an
  exit before the boot's automatic still sends it.
- Failures fall back to SYSCON: a full/manual set that fails, or that the
  confirmed get reads back wrong, is followed by automatic; a refused set
  (REQUEST_ERROR: no fan rights for this lpar) leaves only automatic
  allowed for the boot; a thermal alert event hands a manual duty back to
  SYSCON and refuses manual until the zone clears. The temperatures keep
  working whatever happens to the fan.

Bytes: the set is ps3vuart-tools' set_fan_policy (sguerrini97): header
with payload size 8, tag 0, then version 1, id 0, policy, duty and zeros,
24 bytes. No public source shows the get's bytes: it is sent like the
temperature request (version 1, id 0, two zero bytes, payload size 4), and
its reply is read as version, id, status, policy, speed, duty only once
that layout is confirmed (policy 1, 8 or more bytes, right after setting
automatic); until then reads are logged raw and used for nothing. Replies
are taken with service id 25/26 (set) or 50/51 (get; the temperature
reply comes as 14 to a 13) and 0-16 payload bytes;
the first of each and every REQUEST_ERROR payload are logged. Nothing
else is sent, and SYSCON's EEPROM (its fan tables) is never written.

Known limit: the port's async read wakes at 24 bytes, so a reply shorter
than that is read one message late; such replies never confirm the get
layout.

Forwarded: no (test on the PS3 first; docs/THERMAL.md)

