+ * Fan policy (pwm1, pwm1_enable).
+ *
+ * SYSCON runs the fan from its thermal tables (policy 1, "automatic") unless
+ * a guest sets policy 0 (full speed) or 2 (manual, a fixed duty).  The
+ * setting lives in SYSCON's RAM and outlives the guest that made it: a
+ * manual duty webMAN set in GameOS stays in force under Linux.  So this
+ * driver sets SYSCON automatic at boot, and sets it again on every exit
+ * path (device shutdown before power off, reboot, halt and kexec; the final
+ * power off and restart routines; a panic) whenever it set anything else.
+ * Manual is only a fixed duty, never below PS3_SM_FAN_DUTY_MIN, with no
+ * control loop; a thermal alert with a manual duty in force hands the fan
+ * back to SYSCON.
+ *
+ * Set (service 25): the header (payload_size 8, request_tag 0) and
+ *   u8 version (1), u8 id (0), u8 policy, u8 duty, u8 reserved[4],
+ * 24 bytes on the wire: the set_fan_policy command of ps3vuart-tools
+ * (sguerrini97), whose struct is header + 5 bytes padded to 24, with zeros
+ * where the tool sent stack.  GameOS's sys_sm_set_fan_policy(id, policy,
+ * duty) (lv2 syscall 389) takes the same three values; webMAN-MOD uses
+ * (0, 2, duty) with duty 0x33-0xf4 and hands back with (0, 1, 0).  No
+ * reply is documented: up to PS3_SM_FAN_SET_WAIT_MS is spent waiting for a
+ * REQUEST_ERROR or a reply (service 25 or 26), the first is logged.
+ *
+ * Get (service 50): the header (payload_size 4) and u8 version (1), u8 id
+ * (0), u8 reserved[2], laid out like the temperature request.  No public
+ * tool shows these bytes; sys_sm_get_fan_policy (lv2 syscall 409) returns
+ * status, policy, "mode" (the speed, webMAN reads it as the current duty)
+ * and duty.  The reply (service 51 or 50, 0-16 bytes) is taken as
+ *   u8 version (1), u8 id, u8 status, u8 policy, u8 speed, u8 duty
+ * tentatively: every reply is logged raw at boot, and the layout counts as
+ * confirmed only when it reads policy 1 right after setting SYSCON
+ * automatic, with 8 payload bytes or more (a shorter message stays below
+ * the 24-byte rx trigger and is read one message late).  Until then policy
+ * reads only inform the log; nothing is decided from them.  A get that is
+ * refused or unanswered turns off reads only; a full speed or manual set
+ * that is refused (REQUEST_ERROR: no fan rights for this lpar) turns those
+ * off for the boot and hands the fan back to SYSCON; SYSCON automatic is
+ * still sent on the exit paths, since an earlier setting may have taken.
+ * The temperatures keep working.
+ *
+ * Nothing else is ever sent: no other System Manager service, nothing
+ * that writes SYSCON's EEPROM (the fan tables stay as they are).
